  "worked_solution": "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since ${\u03a6}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is a fundamental matrix for the system, any solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={\u03a6}_{0}(t)\\,c.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{properties of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={\u03a6}_{0}(t)\\,c.\n$$\n\nEvaluating this identity at $t=0$ gives\n\n$$\nx(0)={\u03a6}_{0}(0)c,\n$$\n\nwhich is precisely line $\\text{L3}.$ Thus, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{evaluating both sides at\u00a0}t=0.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L4},$ and $\\text{L5}.$ From $\\text{L3}$ we know\n\n$$\nx(0)={\u03a6}_{0}(0)c,\n$$\n\nand from $\\text{L4}$ we know ${\u03a6}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$ Replacing ${\u03a6}_{0}(0)$ with ${I}_{n}$ in the equation $x(0)={\u03a6}_{0}(0)c$ gives\n\n$$\nx(0)={I}_{n}c=c,\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L5}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{replacing\u00a0}{\u03a6}_{0}(0)\\text{\u00a0with\u00a0}{I}_{n}\\text{\u00a0in\u00a0}x(0)={\u03a6}_{0}(0)c.$",
